Subject: Re: [stan-users] Bayesian model averaging without reversible-jump MCMC on highly-dimensional models
Message-ID: <ixBFDRJj8Y0.BQCo1UcxAQAJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <ixBFDRJj8Y0.Zag10wQlBQAJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
References: <ixBFDRJj8Y0.Zag10wQlBQAJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/stan-users/c/ixBFDRJj8Y0/m/BQCo1UcxAQAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Hello all, thanks again for your comments. Andrew, I wanted to ask what exactly you were thinking, in regards to the case where there are multiple predictions per data point. In particular, I have K=4000 predictions per data point. Were you thinking I would just 'stack' my observations on top of one another, and minimise the sum of squares, or just take the mean/median for each point, and use that instead?

If the latter, was the model below what you were thinking? (Where yPred[i,j] is the average prediction for point i, in model j). Also, would you run this in RStan using: fit <- optimising(belowModel, data)? I have not used this functionality of Stan before.

data {
  int<lower=0> N; ## Total number of observations

  int yTrue[N];
  matrix[N,7] yPred;
  }

parameters {
  simplex lambda[7]; ## Weightings parameters on each model
}

transformed parameters {
  real<lower=0> squared_error;
  squared_error <- dot_self(yTrue - yPred * lambda);
}

model {
  increment_log_prob(-squared_error);
}

generated quantities {
  real<lower=0> sigma_squared;
  sigma_squared <- squared_error / N;
}

