From: ucab04b8f0759c712 at groups.google.com (Daniel Lee)
Date: Mon, 15 Feb 2016 14:00:26 +0000
Subject: Re: [stan-users] Parallel compilation in Stan?
Message-ID: <A6bhLpxvJbg.qLWGgHIAAwAJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <A6bhLpxvJbg.v4dmOMfwAgAJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
References: <A6bhLpxvJbg.vNpbW6pGAgAJ@stan-users.groups.google.com> <A6bhLpxvJbg.v4dmOMfwAgAJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/stan-users/c/A6bhLpxvJbg/m/qLWGgHIAAwAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

It's down to the complexity of the template metaprogramming in C++. It's between the complexity of the Stan math library, Boost, and Eigen.

If you're really interested in finding out what's causing the C++ compiler to be incredibly slow, take a look at the generated C++ header file.

Also, try changing compiler optimization flags. I've seen a lot of variation in compile time (and run time) with the different levels.

Daniel

