Getting back to what's going on:
- Jökull has a 10k line Stan program that is computer generated.
- The stanc compiler correctly translates from Stan to C++.
- He's not able to get the C++ to compile because it takes too long.
- He hasn't gotten to running the program yet.
- He hasn't shared the actual Stan prorgram.
- He's using a Mac with the "standard C++ compiler" (in other words, we don't know what compiler he's using.)
(Jökull, please correct me if I'm wrong)

Jökull, if you're using g++ 4.2, then you're going to get much better performance with clang++ for compilation time. There isn't any blanket advice we can give you -- at this point, you'll have to figure out how the C++ interacts with the compiler you are using. Even point changes in compiler versions have some non-trivial differences in performance.

On Mon, Feb 15, 2016 at 11:05 AM, Bob Carpenter <ca...@alias-i.com> wrote:
> If the model runs fast and compiles slowl, that might
> make sense.  But moving to -O0 will make it compile in
> 1/10th the time but it'll take 10 times longer to run.
>
> How does the C++ header file help understand the compile
> complexity?  Are there flags in the compiler or something?

If the goal is to understand how to do parallel compilation of the C++ (see the subject line), then I think you have to get to the C++ level in order to understand how to deal with the limitations of compilers and complexity of template metaprogramming.

Jökull, if you want more help, here's what you need to provide:
- Mac OS version and compiler version
- the Stan program
- a summary of what the Stan program is / does
(I don't have the time to comb through a 10k computer-generated Stan program unless there's clear guidance.)

Hopefully we can help you get it compiling.

Daniel

