> thanks again for your extremely helpful response. Your suggestions seem to have largely solved the issue - except for the occasional warning message early during the warm-up (about 50 occurences in 4 chains of 100,000 samples, all but one before "[ 10%]  (Warmup)", usually "stan::math::categorical_logit_log: log odds parameter[1] is 1.#QNAN, but must be finite!", or "stan::math::categorical_logit_log: log odds parameter[2] is -1.#INF, but must be finite!"). I have attached my revised R/rstan code below for anyone, who is interested. Next I will try to see whether things stay this stable when I move to the full hierarchical model I intend to use, but as far as I can see this should also work.
>
> As far as I can tell the warnings during warm-up primarily happen when I have exp(-parameter1) or exp(-(parameter1+parameter2)) in the likelihood, in which case when working with the logs of the parameters I still end up using -exp(logparameter1) or exp(log_sum_exp(logparameter1,logparameter2)) in the log-likelihood and presumably there is no way around that.

That can be a problem if you absolutely need something
on the linear rather than log scale and can't keep everything log
all the way to the final log density.

> Other than that I had some silly vectorization related "issues" that must be due to my unfamiliarity with the Stan language. I am a bit puzzled as to whether there is a neater way of writing log_sum_exp(logp[1],log_sum_exp(logp[2],log_sum_exp(logp[4],logp[5]))),

Just log_sum_exp(logp), but that will include every entry in the logp
array.

> do I have to nest log_sum_exp in this way? I also failed to vectorize my conversion of log probabilities to logits (and had to loop over betas[r] <- logp[r] - log1m_exp(logp[r]) for r=1,...,number of categorical outcomes - writing betas <- logp - log1m_exp(logp); got me some errors). On a semi-related note, I have wondered whether there's an equivalent to R's c(1,1,1,1,1), e.g. when I want to specify a dirichlet prior, is there some way of simply writing dirichlet(c(1,1,1,1,1))?

Not yet, but we're working on it.

If you want to do exactly that, you can write dirichlet(rep_vector(1, 5));
Even better, define rep_vector(1, 5) as a transformed data variable so you
don't have to keep recreating it every time it's used.  But if you really
are using 1 as the parameter, you can just drop the dirichlet() because
it's uniform.

- Bob

