From u8aa9b8cc06fdc99c at groups.google.com  Sat Feb 27 07:08:54 2016
From: u8aa9b8cc06fdc99c at groups.google.com (Bob Carpenter)
Date: Sat, 27 Feb 2016 07:08:54 +0000
Subject: Re: [stan-users] log_prob-methods
Message-ID: <TOaN5y3DJ6w.W3JXJ9J0AgAJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <TOaN5y3DJ6w.YsWL3mZZAgAJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
References: <TOaN5y3DJ6w.YsWL3mZZAgAJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/stan-users/c/TOaN5y3DJ6w/m/W3JXJ9J0AgAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

If you want to do HMC (or random-walk Metropolis) in the unconstrained space,
then you need the Jacobians in the gradients and in the Metropolis
accept/reject step.

But, the log densities drop constant terms (additive terms in the log density
only involving constants and data as declared in the data block).

If you really need the log density on the constrained parameters without
the Jacobian, then you have to evaluate that with propto=false in the C++
template to avoid dropping constant terms.  I'm not sure how to do this
through RStan;  internally, Stan doesn't care about the constant terms.

- Bob

